 { The first position I have chosen to illustrate this topic is quite rich, but hopefully not too mindboggling (even if it proved challenging enough for the white player) . White is just winning and, with good calculation, he would be able to seal the game quite quickly. But White did not fully take into account Black’s defensive resources and how to deflate them. Ismael Karim – Abhijeet Gupta, Gibraltar 2012 White has many promising options at this point. The e-pawn is, simply put, very strong. Let us, as an example, say that White plays } ( 27... -- 27. Qc7 Bxf3 29. e7 { which looks completely winning. Black can now try } Kh7 $5 { It is very tempting to simply advance the e-pawn with } 30. e8=Q $2 { and maybe this was indeed what White intended during the game... However, rather than panic and abandon the queen on c7, White could have looked deeper and seen that } ( 29... -- 29. Qc8 $1 { would win the game. Black cannot play ...Re4 and White will queen the pawn. } ) 
 { ...Until he realized that after } Re4 $1 31. Rxe4 fxe4 { things were not so simple! For example: } 32. Qb5 e3 $1 { and it is time for White to pull the emergency brakes with } 33. Qxg7 $1 { with a perpetual check. Or he could have refined the idea with } ) 
 ( 27... -- 27. Qd6 $1 { when Black’s trap is even more useless. After } Bxf3 29. e7 Kh7 { White can safely play } 30. e8=Q $11 { as } Re4 { is met strongly with } 31. Qdg6 $1 { Or there is } ) 
 ( 27... -- 27. Qb8 Kh7 29. Qc8 $1 { when the attack on the bishop is combined with an indirect attack on f5, which we have come to appreciate the importance of. } ) 
 { Actually, White had a lot of ways of winning quickly, but they all involved taking his opponent’s intentions and opportunities into consideration. Had he done this, he would certainly have won and the variations are not that complicated. Awful as it may sound, chances are that he did not even look at attacking the bishop. So, instead of all this, Black was allowed to escape to a lost ending: } 28. e7 $2 Re4 { Like in every line above, this is still the intention. White has not lost all of his advantage, but now he has to prove it all over again. } 29. Rxe4 Qxe4 30. Qxe4 fxe4 31. Ne5 Be8 { This is the last interesting moment for us in this game. At this point, Black wants to play ...g5 and get the king to f6 to get rid of the e7-pawn and activate the bishop. Should he succeed in this he has a fair fighting chance, which is exactly what happened in the game when White played } 32. Nc4 $6 { Eventually Black won. White should have played } ( 32. f4 $1 { Black could then either give White a tempo with: } exf3 { Or he could play } ( 32. Nc4 g5 { as planned. But this also has a drawback. After } 33. fxg5 hxg5 { White will first activate the king and take the b6-pawn with } 34. Kf2 Kg7 35. Nc4 Kf6 36. Ke3 Kxe7 37. Nxb6 ) 
33. Kf2 g5 34. Kxf3 Kg7 35. Ke4 Kf6 36. Nc4 Kxe7 37. Nxb6 Ke6 38. Nc4 { when White is in control and his king very strong. } ) 
 Z0 { Only then will he start to look at taking the e- or a-pawn, giving him an extra pawn, as well as the very handy opportunity to play h2-h4 and create a passed h-pawn, a drawback of 32...g5 Black would just have to live with. I know this last point is more strategic than calculation, but then chess is not really 99% tactics, although chess strategy does rely heavily on tactics. } 
