 { Svidler – Caruana, Amsterdam 2010 } { _ } 24. Bxd5 $1 { The winning line was rather nice. Svidler played too fast because he wanted to catch a movie with his wife and children. Had he been fully focused he would not have made a mess of things here. } ( 24. Ba4 $2 Bxa4 25. Rxe7 { This combination looks absolutely winning, but Black has a fine counterblow. } Bxc2 $1 { Svidler had missed this. Now he has to be a bit careful in order to save the draw. } 26. Rxe8 Qxe8 27. Qg3 Bg6 28. h4 Qe5 29. Qg4 f5 30. Qa4 f6 31. Qa8 Be8 32. h5 f4 33. Qb7 f5 34. b4 c4 35. b5 c3 36. Qc8 f3 ( 36. Bxd5 c2 37. Qxc2 Qe6 38. Qc5 $1 Qxh6 39. Qxd5 Kg7 40. Qe5 Kf7 41. Qxf5 { and White will eventually make the draw after all. } ) 
37. Qc5 Qe1 38. Kh2 Qh4 39. Kg1 Qe1 40. Kh2 Qh4 41. Kg1 Qe1 { 1/2-1/2 } ) 
Bf5 ( 24... Bg4 { is easily refuted, for example by } 25. Bxf7 Kxf7 26. Qxh7 Ke6 27. Qe4 { with a simple win. } ) 
25. Qg3+ $1 ( 25. Bxf7 { secures some advantage, but probably not enough to win. And } ( 25. Rxe7 $2 Qxe7 26. Qxf5 { looks tempting too, but Black wins with } Qe1 27. Qf1 Qh4 $1 { with a strong triple threat. } ) 
 ) 
Bg6 26. Bxf7+ $1 { Pretty, but not the only way. } ( 26. Ra8 { gives a pleasant position after the forced } Nc8 { As White would win instantly after } ( 26. Bxf7+ Qd7 $2 27. Qd6 $1 ) 
 ) 
Kxf7 27. Qb3+ { Black is mated. } 
