 { Adams – Ivanchuk, Porto Carras 2011 } { _ } 34. { -KEY- } Kh2 $3 { The game continuation is not bad, but it is not the most convincing. I was there watching the game live and should say that Adams did not have a lot of time left, just a few minutes, while Ivanchuk had plenty. However, Ivanchuk was playing quickly and did not seriously try to find a defence, which no doubt influenced the level of concentration Mickey was able to display as well. Chess is, after all, a practical game – we need to make many good decisions in a short space of time. In training, we can refine the tools needed to do so, but when we are playing we have to cut corners more often than we (and especially the engines) would like. } ( 34. Qe5 $6 Rxb7 35. Ra8 Be8 36. Rxe8 Kf7 37. Rh8 Qb5 38. Qd6 $1 { Not the strongest according to the engine. However, } ( 38. Rxh7 $2 Kg6 39. Rxb7 Qxb7 40. Qxe6 Kg7 41. Qxf5 Qb4 { would give Black counterplay with the e-pawn. } ) 
Qb2 $1 { The best defence. In the game Ivanchuk went down quickly - in time as well as number of moves, as he did not hesitate with his 'defence' – after } ( 38. Kh2 Qb4 $2 39. Qd8 $1 Qb2 40. Rf8 Kg7 41. Rg8 { 1-0 } ) 
39. Qf8 ( 39. Rxh7 Kg6 { is again not clear. } ) 
Kg6 40. Qe8 Kf6 41. Qd8 Re7 42. Rf8 Kg6 { Had the game taken this direction, Adams would surely have found the winning move here. } 43. Kh2 $3 Qb7 44. Rg8 Kf6 45. Qd4 e5 46. fxe5 Kf7 47. Qc4 Re6 48. Rh8 Kg7 49. Rxh7 Kxh7 50. Qxe6 { White should win this endgame, as he now has a passed pawn of his own. Still, it would take some work: } c5 51. Qxf5 Kg8 52. h4 c4 53. e6 Qc7 54. Kh3 c3 55. Qg6 Qg7 56. Qxe4 Qe7 57. h5 { and the smoke has not fully cleared, but White wins all the same. A weaker option for White is } ) 
 ( 34. Qb6 $2 e3 35. Qc7 Rxb7 $1 36. Qxb7 Qxf4 { which would allow Black to escape with a draw. If White threatens anything, Black gives a perpetual. } ) 
 { The correct choice is: 34 Kh2!! This prophylactic move is essential, now or later. We saw it played later in the above line, but it is even better to play it now. Black loses the chance to play ...Qf1+ gaining a tempo at an important point. } e3 { Other moves do not save Black either: } ( 34... Qd5 35. Ra8 Qd8 36. Qa1 { and 37 Qa7, winning. } ( 34... Be8 35. Ra8 e3 36. Qe5 { simply transposes. } ) 
 ) 
35. Qe5 Rxb7 36. Ra8+ Be8 37. Rxe8+ Kf7 38. Rh8 Qb4 $1 { The toughest defence. } ( 38... Qb5 39. Qd4 Qb2 40. Qd8 { and White wins. } ) 
 { Compared to the game Black is lacking his counterplay. } 39. Rxh7+ Kg6 40. Rh8 Kf7 { Otherwise, Rg8+ kills. } 41. Qxe3 { White is simply winning here. The equal material is far less important than the endangered black king. } c5 ( 41... Qb2 42. Rh7 Kf8 43. Qc5 Kg8 44. Rh6 { and White wins. Best play is apparently } Rh7 45. Rxe6 Rf7 46. Rg6 Kh8 47. Qxc6 { I guess the picture is quite clear here. } ) 
42. Qg3 Ke7 43. Qg7+ Kd6 44. Qe5+ Kc6 45. Qxe6+ Kb5 46. Qxf5 { White wins. } 
